From ced73f8b6566dd09ec6ce60747254c78f8c3bac5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Carson Daniel <wcarsond@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 02:29:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/12] vulkan : two-device all-reduce ordered by host counters
 and VkEvents

The relay mode still paid a scheduler round trip per exchange: a submission
that waits on a semaphore is held back by the OS until the signal, and its
wake-up is tens of microseconds. Now each device bumps a "written" and a
"read" counter in a page of host memory with vkCmdFillBuffer after its copy;
the relay thread reads the counters (host RAM, nanoseconds) and sets a VkEvent
on the other device, whose queue waits for it with vkCmdWaitEvents inside the
already-running submission. No semaphore waits remain. The relay mode stays
behind GGML_VK_COMM_RELAY=1 and the one-stop mode behind GGML_VK_COMM_SYNC=1.

ggml_vk_buffer_from_host_ptr gains a `cached` parameter; the all-reduce's host
memory uses the memory type without HOST_CACHED.

Tried and dropped: a one-thread shader polling the counters. On the Intel
Windows driver a device's polls of the other device's writes return a stale
value for as long as the poll lasts, with volatile loads, with atomics and on
the uncached memory type alike; the first token worked only because warm-up
allocations kept invalidating caches. Vulkan external semaphores import
between the two physical devices but share nothing (vk-semaphore-probe).

Measured on two Arc Pro B60s, Windows 11, vs one card (tg128 / pp512):
  granite 30B dense     relay 1.24x / 1.49x -> events 1.47x / 1.51x
  qwen2.5-coder 14B     relay 1.03x / 1.26x -> events 1.30x / 1.28x
  qwen3 30B-A3B MoE     relay 0.64x / 1.23x -> events 0.84x / 1.24x
Output is byte-identical to the generic path on a 500-token greedy completion.
